 The Sheets pane. The grid renders and the totals are real (the formulas
 evaluate through CellEval in the order CellGraph derives); nothing here
 takes a keystroke or a click yet, which is the rest of SHEET-10.

 ONE LABEL PER CELL, NOT ONE PER ROW. `sheet-render` pads a row with
 spaces and lines the columns up only in a monospace font, which the desk
 does not promise; a row of labels each given the same minimum width
 lines up whatever the font's metrics are. The text of each cell still
 comes from the same evaluator the text renderer uses, so the pane and
 `sheet-render` can never disagree about a value.

 THE TREE IS REBUILT EVERY REPAINT, which is the Clock's cost model
 rather than a leak: `desk-loop` repaints on an event or a tick, and
 every allocation a pane makes after `desk-run` took the base mark is
 released when the pane exits. THE SHEET IS NOT. It is a field of
 `SheetPane`, built once, because a rebuilt sheet discards the edit that
 was typed into it.

Section: The Pane's State

  SheetPane = record {
    sp-sheet : Sheet,
    sp-row : Integer,
    sp-col : Integer,
    sp-buf : Text
  }

 `sp-row` and `sp-col` are the focused cell and `sp-buf` is what has been
 typed into it so far. Nothing paints either yet: the hit test that moves
 the focus and the keystrokes that fill the buffer are the two SHEET-10
 steps after this one, and a highlight painted before a click can move it
 would show a focus a person cannot change.

  gsh-pane-new : Integer -> SheetPane
  gsh-pane-new (dummy) =
    SheetPane { sp-sheet = gsh-demo 0, sp-row = 1, sp-col = 0, sp-buf = "" }

 THE BUFFER IS BUILT AS BYTES AND NOT WITH `char-code`. A keystroke
 arrives as an ASCII code point from `apply-mods`, which is an I/O
 boundary, so it crosses into text as a UTF-8 byte; `char-code` answers a
 CCE code point and would put a different character in the cell.

  gsh-buf-add : Text, Integer -> Text
  gsh-buf-add (buf) (ch) = buf & utf8-bytes-to-text (list-push [] ch)

  gsh-buf-back : Text -> Text
  gsh-buf-back (buf) =
    let bs = text-to-utf8-bytes buf
    in let n = list-length bs
    in if n <= 0 then buf
    else utf8-bytes-to-text (ev-slice bs 0 (n - 1) [])

 COMMITTING GOES THROUGH `sheet-type`, which is the one place that decides
 what a person typed IS: text opening with `=` is a formula, digits are a
 number, nothing typed clears the cell. A second rule here would be a
 second answer to the same question.

  gsh-commit : SheetPane -> Sheet
  gsh-commit (sp) = sheet-type (sp.sp-sheet) (sp.sp-row) (sp.sp-col) (sp.sp-buf)

Section: The Sheet This Pane Shows

 A worked example rather than an empty grid, because an empty grid cannot
 show that the totals are computed: D2 and D3 are products, D4 is a SUM
 over them, and D5 asks for a total of a text cell and is REFUSED by name.

  gsh-rows-shown : Integer = 6
  gsh-cols-shown : Integer = 4
  gsh-cell-w : Integer = 92
  gsh-cell-h : Integer = 20

  gsh-demo : Integer -> Sheet
  gsh-demo (dummy) =
    let s0 = sheet-open gsh-rows-shown gsh-cols-shown 512
    in let s1 = sheet-type (sheet-type s0 0 0 "Item") 0 1 "Qty"
    in let s2 = sheet-type (sheet-type s1 0 2 "Cost") 0 3 "Total"
    in let s3 = sheet-type (sheet-type s2 1 0 "Widget") 1 1 "3"
    in let s4 = sheet-type (sheet-type s3 1 2 "250") 1 3 "=B2*C2"
    in let s5 = sheet-type (sheet-type s4 2 0 "Gadget") 2 1 "2"
    in let s6 = sheet-type (sheet-type s5 2 2 "400") 2 3 "=B3*C3"
    in let s7 = sheet-type (sheet-type s6 3 0 "Total") 3 3 "=SUM(D2:D3)"
    in sheet-type (sheet-type s7 4 0 "Refused") 4 3 "=B2+A2"

Section: The Tree

 THE CELL TEXT COMES FROM CellView'S FITTING, NOT FROM `eval-cell`, so
 the pane keeps the rules the app claims: a number too wide for its
 column fills with `#` rather than being cut, because a cut number reads
 as a different number and nothing in the display says so, and a cut text
 carries a `>` in its last position. Building labels from the raw value
 text let a refusal clip silently, which a photograph found and every
 headless arm had passed.

 The width is in CHARACTERS because that is what `vw-cell` fits to, and
 the column is `gsh-cell-w` LOGICAL pixels at 8 pixels a character in the
 desk's base font.

  gsh-cell-chars : Integer
  gsh-cell-chars = gsh-cell-w / 8

 THE ID IS BUILT IN ONE PLACE AND READ BACK IN ONE PLACE. `gsh-cell-id`
 is what the painter names a cell with and what the hit test decodes, so
 the two cannot drift into naming different cells; `gsh-cell-at` walks
 the same range the painter walks rather than parsing the text back.

  gsh-cell-id : Integer, Integer -> Text
  gsh-cell-id (r) (c) = "sh-c-" & show r & "-" & show c

  gsh-cell-at : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gsh-cell-at (id) (r) (c) =
    if r >= gsh-rows-shown then 0 - 1
    else if c >= gsh-cols-shown then gsh-cell-at id (r + 1) 0
    else if id == gsh-cell-id r c then r * gsh-cols-shown + c
    else gsh-cell-at id r (c + 1)

 A CELL BEING TYPED INTO SHOWS WHAT IS BEING TYPED, not the value under
 it. Until Enter commits, the buffer is the only place the keystrokes
 exist, so a focused cell with a non-empty buffer renders the buffer,
 left-aligned the way text is; committing clears the buffer and the cell
 goes back to showing what the evaluator says.

  gsh-cell-text : Sheet, Results, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  gsh-cell-text (sh) (rs) (r) (c) (fr) (fc) (buf) =
    if r == fr & c == fc & vw-len buf > 0 then vw-left-of buf gsh-cell-chars
    else vw-cell sh rs r c gsh-cell-chars

  gsh-cell-label : Sheet, Results, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> WidgetNode
  gsh-cell-label (sh) (rs) (r) (c) (fr) (fc) (buf) =
    let base = widget-set-min (widget-label (gsh-cell-id r c) (gsh-cell-text sh rs r c fr fc buf)) gsh-cell-w gsh-cell-h
    in if r == fr & c == fc then widget-set-tone base tone-primary else base

  gsh-row-cells : Sheet, Results, Integer, Integer, List WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, Text -> List WidgetNode
  gsh-row-cells (sh) (rs) (r) (c) (acc) (fr) (fc) (buf) =
    if c >= gsh-cols-shown then acc
    else gsh-row-cells sh rs r (c + 1) (list-push acc (gsh-cell-label sh rs r c fr fc buf)) fr fc buf

  gsh-row : Sheet, Results, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> WidgetNode
  gsh-row (sh) (rs) (r) (fr) (fc) (buf) =
    widget-set-min (widget-panel ("sh-r-" & show r) DirRow 4 (gsh-row-cells sh rs r 0 [] fr fc buf)) 0 gsh-cell-h

  gsh-rows : Sheet, Results, Integer, List WidgetNode, Integer, Integer, Text -> List WidgetNode
  gsh-rows (sh) (rs) (r) (acc) (fr) (fc) (buf) =
    if r >= gsh-rows-shown then acc
    else gsh-rows sh rs (r + 1) (list-push acc (gsh-row sh rs r fr fc buf)) fr fc buf

 The column letters are a heading row of their own rather than the
 sheet's first row, because a person reads A, B, C to name a cell and the
 sheet's own first row is the table's headings.

  gsh-head-cells : Integer, List WidgetNode -> List WidgetNode
  gsh-head-cells (c) (acc) =
    if c >= gsh-cols-shown then acc
    else gsh-head-cells (c + 1) (list-push acc (widget-set-min (widget-label ("sh-h-" & show c) (gr-col-name c)) gsh-cell-w gsh-cell-h))

  gsh-head : Integer -> WidgetNode
  gsh-head (dummy) =
    widget-set-min (widget-panel "sh-head" DirRow 4 (gsh-head-cells 0 [])) 0 gsh-cell-h

 THE SLACK GOES BELOW THE GRID, NOT THROUGH IT. A panel is born
 flexible, so the grid took every spare pixel of the window and laid its
 six rows down a box far taller than they need: the column letters sat at
 the top, the data sat at the bottom, and a dead band split them. Photo-
 graphed 2026-09-08 and invisible to every headless arm, which is why a
 pane's layout is looked at and not only asserted.

 The grid therefore takes flex ZERO and a minimum of its own rows, which
 is the pairing the desk contract requires: flex 0 alone is "no share of
 the main axis", and a node with no min size collapses to nothing rather
 than sizing to its content. A `widget-spacer` below it carries the flex
 instead, so every spare pixel lands under the last row.

 THE SLACK CARRIER MUST NOT PAINT, AND AN EMPTY PANEL DOES. A panel fills
 its own box with the theme's panel background, which under a gradient
 restarts the gradient at the carrier's own top. Photographed 2026-09-08 at
 1600x900: a seam at y 684 where the last grid row ends, the shade lifting
 back to 001428 and fading to 000E1C over the 141 rows below it, against a
 control that carried the window's background there. A spacer resolves to
 `widget-style-bare` and all three renderers answer it without drawing.

  gsh-grid-h : Integer
  gsh-grid-h = gsh-rows-shown * gsh-cell-h + (gsh-rows-shown - 1) * 2

  gsh-tree : SheetPane -> WidgetNode
  gsh-tree (sp) =
    let sh = sp.sp-sheet
    in let rs = eval-run (sh.sh-prog)
    in let grid = widget-set-min (widget-set-flex (widget-panel "sh-grid" DirColumn 2 (gsh-rows sh rs 0 [] (sp.sp-row) (sp.sp-col) (sp.sp-buf))) 0) 0 gsh-grid-h
    in let slack = widget-spacer "sh-slack"
    in widget-panel "sh-root" DirColumn 4 (list-push (list-push (list-push (list-push [] (gsh-head 0)) (widget-separator "sh-sep")) grid) slack)

